-<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="Unified Dynamics public research atlas: finite tetrahedral mathematics, declared dynamics, source provenance and independently testable physical questions."><meta name="theme-color" content="#f7f6ef"><title>Updates · Unified Dynamics</title><link rel="icon" href="/assets/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/style.css"><script src="/assets/app.js" defer></script></head><body data-page="updates"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><aside class="sidebar"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 3L3 33H37Z M20 3L22 23L3 33 M22 23L37 33"/></svg><div>Unified<br>Dynamics<small>PUBLIC RESEARCH ATLAS</small></div></a><button class="menu-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="navigation">Explore <span>☰</span></button><nav id="navigation" aria-label="Primary"><a href="/" ><span>01</span>Overview</a><a href="/primer/" ><span>02</span>Primer</a><a href="/mathematics/" ><span>03</span>Mathematics</a><a href="/dynamics/" ><span>04</span>Dynamics</a><a href="/evidence/" ><span>05</span>Evidence</a><a href="/papers/" ><span>06</span>Papers</a><a href="/sources/" ><span>07</span>Sources</a><a href="/history/" ><span>08</span>History</a><a href="/contribution/" ><span>09</span>Contribution</a><a href="/updates/" aria-current="page"><span>10</span>Updates</a><a href="/reconciliation/"><span>11</span>Reconciliation</a><a href="/dependencies/"><span>12</span>Dependencies</a><a href="/source-contract/"><span>13</span>Source contract</a><a href="/source-programme/"><span>14</span>Source programme</a><a href="/realization/"><span>15</span>Realization</a><a href="/joint-carrier/"><span>16</span>Joint carrier</a><a href="/programmed-clock/"><span>17</span>Programmed clock</a><a href="/native-admission/"><span>18</span>Native admission</a><a href="/metric-admission/"><span>19</span>Source metrics</a><a href="/subdivision/"><span>20</span>Subdivision</a><a href="/metric-selection/"><span>21</span>Metric selection</a><a href="/refinement-tower/"><span>22</span>Refinement tower</a><a href="/history-comparison/"><span>23</span>History comparison</a></nav><div class="sidebar-bottom"><span class="status-dot"></span> Physical promotion <b>0</b><p>Independent human review<br>pending</p><a href="https://github.com/ud-tetra/UD">Research repository ↗</a></div></aside><div class="page-shell"><header class="topbar"><span>FINITE STRUCTURE / OPEN CORRESPONDENCE</span><a href="/sources/">Search the record <span>⌕</span></a></header><main id="main"><div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">Updates and next work</div><h1>A methodical consolidation.</h1><p class="lede">Dated development notes live here. Current source versions, unresolved coverage and next gates stay visible.</p></div><article class="claim" id="finite-rank-v01"><div class="eyebrow">16 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The finite-offset full-rank stability gap is closed</h2><p>All six balanced phase-branch embeddings retain nonlinear orbital stability at every fixed finite rank across the certified interval 0&lt;ε≤10⁻⁵. A matter-aligned normal gauge cancels the mixed matter–color Hessian term exactly. A two-color determinant identity bounds the magnetic correction and leaves explicit positive color and vacuum frequency gaps. The twelve and eight scalar saddle embeddings remain unstable.</p><p>The constructor replay passed 28 exact/rigorous interval checks. Four floating full-matrix synthetic fixtures agree with the quadratic formula to within 8×10⁻⁸; these are numerical diagnostics, with no experimental samples. The stability neighborhood is local and is not given an explicit displacement radius. The coefficient interval is sufficient, not maximal; the residual global stabilizer moment-map condition is retained. This milestone supersedes the open full-rank finite-offset stability slot in G12. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#finite-rank-v01">Read the full-rank finite-offset theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="finite-offset-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Near-threshold existence now has a numerical validity region</h2><p>A regularized full stationary system certifies the 26 phase branches on 0&lt;ε≤10⁻⁵. The certificate covers the rescaled zero endpoint, all scalar Morse indices and explicit amplitude and loop-readout errors. It also gives sufficient deterministic noise budgets for both oriented phases and six labeled real loop amplitudes.</p><p>Exact symmetry sectors and scaled Schur complements bound the stable soft-mode frequency ratio to within 0.005 of its limiting value when a=N=r=1. The proof replay passed 1,536 nested interval boxes and 2,048 spectral cut tests. The interval is sufficient, not maximal; uniqueness is within the certified tubes. Higher-rank stationary embeddings are covered, while their full normal stability radius remains open. No experimental data are analyzed. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#finite-offset-v01">Read the finite-offset theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="branch-readout-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Branch observables and a bounded-error measurement design</h2><p>A matter-dressed Wilson observable is invariant under the full local U(r) gauge group. On the one-active-color branch embeddings its oriented phases recover the critical vector exactly, because the nonlinear fast correction cancels. Fourth-moment fingerprints distinguish the three families and a signed cubic separates the two body-diagonal permutation orbits.</p><p>The theorem proves a sufficient phase-noise margin and an exact information limit for real-only channels. A proposed measurement contract specifies phase, Gauss, calibration and small-response requirements. Two primary studies offer gauge or holonomy readout precedents; their different actions and geometries do not currently provide a direct UD branch test. This is a limited compatibility screen, with no empirical reanalysis. The frozen action and ledger are unchanged; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#branch-readout-v01">Read the branch-readout theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="critical-bifurcation-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>New stable phase equilibria appear beyond the curved-orbit threshold</h2><p>The exact stationary energy yields six balanced minima, twelve index-one saddles and eight index-two saddles for sufficiently small positive coefficient excess. These counts retain vertex labels and describe phase-amplitude patterns on the fixed carrier. The scalar local diagram is complete; the higher-rank theorem concerns embeddings of these branches.</p><p>A reversing symmetry supplies the exact scalar instability counts and leading soft frequencies, including Gauss backreaction. The balanced branches persist as full-rank stable orbits near threshold, while the original orbit remains unstable above its previous boundary. Energy conservation rules out attraction to the minima; a sourced preparation or selection law remains open. 66 exact constructor checks and twelve full stationary-energy solutions with 42-pair spectra passed. No numerical bound on the theorem's neighborhood radius or new dynamical trajectory is claimed. Independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#critical-bifurcation-v01">Read the bifurcation theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="residual-gauss-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The final residual Gauss constraint is explicit</h2><p>The extra-color oscillator list now has its admissibility rule: opposite oscillator orientations must balance in total amplitude, and the full matrix charge must cancel with the unused vacuum commutators. A weighted-shift construction completes every trace-balanced color state with one vacuum oscillator, with an energy bound that is exact at r=3.</p><p>At r=2 the color quotient is homeomorphic to the cone of complex rank-one 28×28 matrices; its inherited reduced Poisson structure is retained. The full open stratum has 222 real dimensions and the full stabilizer stratum 112. A free zero-moment witness gives principal dimension 26r²+60r-2 at every finite rank. The standard local symplectic reduction theorem supplies the exact constraint germ; displayed observable frequencies are quadratic dynamics only. 70 exact checks and a full matrix cubic-error diagnostic passed. The previous stability interval is unchanged. Independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#residual-gauss-v01">Read the residual Gauss theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="higher-rank-curved-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Higher-rank color stability closes for the embedded family</h2><p>The additional color slice has positive mass and matter stiffness. Its magnetic Hessian vanishes by an exact two-color determinant identity. Positive unused-block cycle stiffness then extends the scalar energy minimum, including its quartic endpoint, to all finite ranks.</p><p>For r≥2 the sharp interval is 0&lt;κ≤rκ*(N). Zero stiffness is unstable by exact unused-color holonomy drift, whereas the scalar r=1 interval still includes zero. The proof covers the full fixed-norm surface without assuming a smooth residual gauge quotient. 57 exact constructor checks, rank-two/rank-three matrix fixtures and two full nonlinear rank-two campaigns passed. Unchanged candidate action; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#higher-rank-curved-v01">Read the full-rank stability theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="curved-modes-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The scalar curved branch now has a sharp stability boundary</h2><p>Gauss and phase reduction split the fixed-norm scalar dynamics into 14 neutral matter oscillator pairs and 28 coupled link–charge pairs. Exact symmetry sections specify every frequency and all five stiffness crossings. The first crossing gives κ*/N≈0.03760248457, about 29% above the earlier conservative bound.</p><p>The orbit remains nonlinearly stable at equality: a matter-relaxed quartic is positive, with its two symmetric coefficients rigorously enclosed by integer arithmetic. Above the boundary a real growing mode produces constrained nonlinear departure. The action is unchanged; additional color directions at higher rank remain open. 47 exact checks, four numerical linear benchmarks and three nonlinear campaigns; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#curved-modes-v01">Read the reduced modes and endpoint proof →</a></article><article class="claim" id="curved-ground-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>A stable matter-bearing background in the same action</h2><p>The completed flat-family obstruction led to a different exact branch: U=-ib, E=0 and equal positive lowest-mode matter, with every diamond holonomy -1. The scalar orbit is nonlinearly stable for 0≤κ&lt;Nρ(10-√10)/(120(6+√2)), where ρ=√(8+2√10) and each species norm is N. The bound is sufficient and conservative.</p><p>A separate compactness argument establishes a stable global minimizing set at every finite rank. It proves neither individual-orbit stability at higher rank nor attraction. The old flat-family instability is preserved. 40 exact constructor checks and two finite nonlinear numerical campaigns passed; no experimental data. Independent review pending, physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#curved-ground-v01">Read the curved-orbit stability theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="resonance-closure-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The six open resonances now have nonlinear witnesses</h2><p>Exact fixed-point sectors of the full nonlinear flow exclude the resonant modes while retaining a nonzero averaged matter drift. S3 closes t=2/3, 8/3 and 16/(4±√2); C4 closes 16/(6±√2). No detuning or action change is used.</p><p>The nonzero neutral flat family is therefore nonlinearly orbitally unstable across a&gt;0, κ≥0 at finite rank, conditional on the declared action and the preceding theorem. Positive-stiffness empty vacuum stability is preserved; other backgrounds remain unclassified. 104 exact checks and six numerical campaigns; independent review pending; physical promotion 0. Earlier dated entries below preserve their historical boundaries.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#resonance-closure-v01">Read the six-case closure theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="nonlinear-orbital-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>A nonlinear departure theorem with six open resonances</h2><p>Within the unchanged candidate action, fixed-norm Gauss-admissible perturbations can approach the nonzero flat stationary orbit while exact trajectories reach fixed higher-simplex matter stock on O(1/ε) time. The proof uses conserved co-moving matter coherence, exact Coulomb dynamics and a finite-Fourier averaging estimate; four field resonances have separate force-canceling constructions.</p><p>Six positive values of t=aκ/r remain open: 2/3, 8/3, 16/(6±√2), and 16/(4±√2). Zero stiffness instead has an exact holonomy drift counterexample. The positive-stiffness empty vacuum remains stable. 42 exact constructor checks and three numerical campaigns passed; no experimental data or physical promotion.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#nonlinear-orbital-v01">Read the theorem and its parameter boundary →</a></article><article class="claim" id="gauge-reduced-modes-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Imaginary frequencies can retain secular growth</h2><p>The exact Gauss reduction leaves 14 link oscillator pairs, 28 algebraic matter pairs and one neutral zero pair. Five positive link stiffnesses are 6, 6±√2 and 4±√2. The nonzero neutral flat family has persistent nongauge Jordan chains, including at isolated forcing cancellations and field resonances. Positive magnetic stiffness alone does not make that family linearly bounded.</p><p>The empty vacuum gauge orbit is separately proved nonlinearly Lyapunov stable for positive stiffness; zero stiffness admits exact drift instability. Nonlinear stability of the nonzero family remains open. 50 exact constructor checks passed; no experimental data were used. Physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#gauge-reduced-modes-v01">Read the reduction and stability theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="joint-hamiltonian-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Neutral matter can drive initially flat links</h2><p>The declared joint Hamiltonian has first-class Gauss constraints, conserved energy and matter norms, reciprocal backreaction and global temporal-gauge solutions. A single canonical fundamental species fails closed full-U(r) neutrality; an explicit opposite-charge dynamical partner repairs that branch. A neutral two-node scalar preparation gives diamond phase 2as² + O(s³) and curvature defect 2a²s⁴ + O(s⁵).</p><p>28 exact constructor checks and a separately typed numerical replay passed. This is conditional dynamics under an added action and species, with independent review pending and physical promotion 0. Next gate: admissible neutral stationary backgrounds and gauge-reduced modes.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#joint-hamiltonian-v01">Read the theorem and replay →</a></article><div class="release-note"><span class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 UTC · Atlas 0.2</span><h2>Claim reconciliation and substantive source views</h2><p>Compared all 448 later ledger rows with 235 historical rows; linked ten versioned dispositions and current reuse limits. Replaced all 65 empty source views with source-linked reconstructions or detailed source-blocked audit notes. Historical originals and source bins remain preserved.</p><p>The Gap 2 running sign conflict, conditional idle-response index, retired scalar action, sidecar custody, strict interface and write/storage timing are explicit. Proof DAG and independent physical correspondence remain open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/reconciliation/">Read the reconciliation →</a> · <a class="text-link" href="/releases/UD_RECONSTRUCTED_SOURCE_VIEWS_v0.2.zip" download>Substantive source archive ↓</a> · <a class="text-link" href="/releases/UD_PUBLIC_ATLAS_v0.2_RELEASE.zip" download>Download reading edition 0.2 ↓</a></div><div class="release-note"><span class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 UTC · Atlas 0.1</span><h2>A fresh public presentation layer</h2><p>Four review papers, 42 repository papers/reports, 977 searchable portal records, 5,520 hashed portal files, 20 available attachments and a scientific memory crosswalk are connected in one navigation system.</p><p>This is an initial consolidated reading edition. Catalog coverage is broader than proof audit coverage. No scientific claim or physical correspondence is promoted by this release.</p><a class="text-link" href="/assets/coverage.json">Machine-readable coverage record ↓</a> · <a class="text-link" href="/releases/UD_PUBLIC_ATLAS_v0.1_RELEASE.zip" download>Download this reading edition ↓</a></div><article class="claim" id="history-comparison-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October2026 · Reading edition0.14</div><h2>Different histories require more than matching frequencies</h2><p>The star29 and barycentric149 carriers agree on the old code. An unrestricted full-generator star embedding exists, but S4-equivariance fails because its invariant √5 pair is absent from the target. A163-direction retained-record completion avoids losing old detail. Exact inherited skew dynamics cannot populate initially blank details; native excitation remains OPEN.</p><a class="text-link" href="/history-comparison/">Inspect history and source gates →</a></article><article class="claim" id="refinement-tower-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition0.13</div><h2>Refinement details need inherited calibration</h2><p>The two-level15→149→2745 tower has an exact edge-detail mismatch2 versus4 under sequential versus fresh final calibration. Transporting each level detail pairing restores consistency for regrouping the same sequence. Coarse observations compose,17 complete effects retain both detail outcomes, and full carrier growth is explicit. Native history and physical adoption remain open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/refinement-tower/">Inspect refinement coherence →</a></article><article class="claim" id="metric-selection-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition0.12</div><h2>Metric and observable selection has explicit premises</h2><p>Native fixed-carrier fidelity selectsI and coordinate stocks. Across ordinary subdivision, exact metric and observer witnesses prove remaining freedom, and arbitrary diagonal fine metrics fail full generator inheritance. Affine readout, calibrated orthogonal residual and blind observation uniquely select a conditional extension; adoption remains open.</p><a class="text-link" href="/metric-selection/">Inspect metric and observer selection →</a></article><article class="claim" id="subdivision-v01"><div class="eyebrow">14 October 2026 · Reading edition 0.11</div><h2>Subdivision has three separate compatibility tests</h2><p>The