+<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="Unified Dynamics public research atlas: finite tetrahedral mathematics, declared dynamics, source provenance and independently testable physical questions."><meta name="theme-color" content="#f7f6ef"><title>Updates · Unified Dynamics</title><link rel="icon" href="/assets/icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/style.css"><script src="/assets/app.js" defer></script></head><body data-page="updates"><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a><aside class="sidebar"><a class="brand" href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 3L3 33H37Z M20 3L22 23L3 33 M22 23L37 33"/></svg><div>Unified<br>Dynamics<small>PUBLIC RESEARCH ATLAS</small></div></a><button class="menu-toggle" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="navigation">Explore <span>☰</span></button><nav id="navigation" aria-label="Primary"><a href="/" ><span>01</span>Overview</a><a href="/primer/" ><span>02</span>Primer</a><a href="/mathematics/" ><span>03</span>Mathematics</a><a href="/dynamics/" ><span>04</span>Dynamics</a><a href="/evidence/" ><span>05</span>Evidence</a><a href="/papers/" ><span>06</span>Papers</a><a href="/sources/" ><span>07</span>Sources</a><a href="/history/" ><span>08</span>History</a><a href="/contribution/" ><span>09</span>Contribution</a><a href="/updates/" aria-current="page"><span>10</span>Updates</a><a href="/reconciliation/"><span>11</span>Reconciliation</a><a href="/dependencies/"><span>12</span>Dependencies</a><a href="/source-contract/"><span>13</span>Source contract</a><a href="/source-programme/"><span>14</span>Source programme</a><a href="/realization/"><span>15</span>Realization</a><a href="/joint-carrier/"><span>16</span>Joint carrier</a><a href="/programmed-clock/"><span>17</span>Programmed clock</a><a href="/native-admission/"><span>18</span>Native admission</a><a href="/metric-admission/"><span>19</span>Source metrics</a><a href="/subdivision/"><span>20</span>Subdivision</a><a href="/metric-selection/"><span>21</span>Metric selection</a><a href="/refinement-tower/"><span>22</span>Refinement tower</a><a href="/history-comparison/"><span>23</span>History comparison</a></nav><div class="sidebar-bottom"><span class="status-dot"></span> Physical promotion <b>0</b><p>Independent human review<br>pending</p><a href="https://github.com/ud-tetra/UD">Research repository ↗</a></div></aside><div class="page-shell"><header class="topbar"><span>FINITE STRUCTURE / OPEN CORRESPONDENCE</span><a href="/sources/">Search the record <span>⌕</span></a></header><main id="main"><div class="page-intro"><div class="eyebrow">Updates and next work</div><h1>A methodical consolidation.</h1><p class="lede">Dated development notes live here. Current source versions, unresolved coverage and next gates stay visible.</p></div><article class="claim" id="energy-barrier-v01"><div class="eyebrow">16 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>A quantitative nonlinear trapping budget is certified</h2><p>The six balanced finite-offset phase branches now have an explicit outer trapping radius, boundary energy barrier and smaller sufficient initial ball at every finite rank. The proof certifies the slow scalar Hessian floor and bounds global second and third geodesic energy derivatives. It uses distance to the compact gauge/species-phase orbit and preserves the residual stabilizer constraint.</p><p>All 211 exact/rigorous interval checks passed across 64 new parameter cells. Six synthetic jet diagnostics passed; no experimental data are used. In a=N=1 at ε=10⁻⁵ the conservative bounds are R≈2.41935×10⁻⁷, B≈1.46332×10⁻¹⁹ and δ≈7.65067×10⁻¹¹ in the declared mathematical normalization. This closes G13’s explicit nonlinear-radius slot for positive offsets. The quartic endpoint needs a separate quantitative modulus; the bounds are not maximal basins or laboratory tolerances. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#energy-barrier-v01">Read the nonlinear energy-barrier theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="finite-rank-v01"><div class="eyebrow">16 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>The finite-offset full-rank stability gap is closed</h2><p>All six balanced phase-branch embeddings retain nonlinear orbital stability at every fixed finite rank across the certified interval 0&lt;ε≤10⁻⁵. A matter-aligned normal gauge cancels the mixed matter–color Hessian term exactly. A two-color determinant identity bounds the magnetic correction and leaves explicit positive color and vacuum frequency gaps. The twelve and eight scalar saddle embeddings remain unstable.</p><p>The constructor replay passed 28 exact/rigorous interval checks. Four floating full-matrix synthetic fixtures agree with the quadratic formula to within 8×10⁻⁸; these are numerical diagnostics, with no experimental samples. The stability neighborhood is local and is not given an explicit displacement radius. The coefficient interval is sufficient, not maximal; the residual global stabilizer moment-map condition is retained. This milestone supersedes the open full-rank finite-offset stability slot in G12. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#finite-rank-v01">Read the full-rank finite-offset theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="finite-offset-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Near-threshold existence now has a numerical validity region</h2><p>A regularized full stationary system certifies the 26 phase branches on 0&lt;ε≤10⁻⁵. The certificate covers the rescaled zero endpoint, all scalar Morse indices and explicit amplitude and loop-readout errors. It also gives sufficient deterministic noise budgets for both oriented phases and six labeled real loop amplitudes.</p><p>Exact symmetry sectors and scaled Schur complements bound the stable soft-mode frequency ratio to within 0.005 of its limiting value when a=N=r=1. The proof replay passed 1,536 nested interval boxes and 2,048 spectral cut tests. The interval is sufficient, not maximal; uniqueness is within the certified tubes. Higher-rank stationary embeddings are covered, while their full normal stability radius remains open. No experimental data are analyzed. Development, no registry entry; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#finite-offset-v01">Read the finite-offset theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="branch-readout-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>Branch observables and a bounded-error measurement design</h2><p>A matter-dressed Wilson observable is invariant under the full local U(r) gauge group. On the one-active-color branch embeddings its oriented phases recover the critical vector exactly, because the nonlinear fast correction cancels. Fourth-moment fingerprints distinguish the three families and a signed cubic separates the two body-diagonal permutation orbits.</p><p>The theorem proves a sufficient phase-noise margin and an exact information limit for real-only channels. A proposed measurement contract specifies phase, Gauss, calibration and small-response requirements. Two primary studies offer gauge or holonomy readout precedents; their different actions and geometries do not currently provide a direct UD branch test. This is a limited compatibility screen, with no empirical reanalysis. The frozen action and ledger are unchanged; independent review pending; physical promotion 0.</p><a class="text-link" href="/papers/#branch-readout-v01">Read the branch-readout theorem →</a></article><article class="claim" id="critical-bifurcation-v01"><div class="eyebrow">15 October 2026 UTC · Theorem milestone 0.1</div><h2>New stable phase equilibria